---
// Sources and licences of the Aeneid Reader (src/pages/sources.astro).
import { READINGS } from "./alignment";
import { LASLA_COMMIT, PERSEUS_COMMIT } from "./sources";

const readings = Object.entries(READINGS).map(([key, lasla]) => {
  const [line, word] = key.split(" ");
  return { line, word, lasla };
});
---
<h2>Latin</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>J. B. Greenough (ed.), <i>The Bucolics, Aeneid, and Georgics of Virgil</i> (Boston: Ginn, 1881)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. The text, line numbers and paragraphs come from the Perseus Digital Library’s
    <a href="https://github.com/PerseusDL/canonical-latinLit">canonical-latinLit</a> edition
    <code>phi0690.phi003.perseus-lat2</code>, pinned to commit <code>{PERSEUS_COMMIT}</code>. Perseus’s encoding
    is licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>; this reader uses only
    Greenough’s words, punctuation, line numbers and paragraph divisions. Lines 426 and 711, which Greenough
    brackets as spurious, are shown in square brackets. Greenough’s speech marks are not printed.
  </dd>
  <dt>LASLA, Université de Liège: lemmatized and tagged Aeneid</dt>
  <dd>
    Every word’s lemma and morphology come from the LASLA corpus as converted to CoNLL-U by CIRCSE
    (<a href="https://github.com/CIRCSE/LASLA">CIRCSE/LASLA</a>, release v1.0.1, commit
    <code>{LASLA_COMMIT}</code>; <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.8370759">Zenodo</a>). LASLA’s data is
    licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>, so this reader’s Latin
    annotation, and the word data built from it, may be reused only for non-commercial purposes, with attribution and
    under the same licence.
  </dd>
  <dt>Morphology</dt>
  <dd>
    LASLA’s Universal Dependencies features are kept, including its declension and conjugation classes. Two
    changes: nouns take their gender from the dictionary, because LASLA files first-declension masculines
    (<i>Aeneas</i>, <i>agricola</i>) as feminine; and verbs whose lemma ends in <i>-or</i> are labelled deponent
    rather than passive. LASLA’s lemmas are lowercase and spell consonantal u and i as <i>u</i> and <i>i</i>; the
    reader shows the matching form of Frieze’s headword instead (<i>uir</i> as <i>vir</i>, <i>cano</i> as
    <i>canō</i>).
  </dd>
  <dt>Aligning Greenough’s text with LASLA’s</dt>
  <dd>
    LASLA follows another edition. Each printed word takes the annotation of the LASLA word in the same position of
    the same line. LASLA’s four spurious opening lines (<i>Ille ego qui quondam…</i>) are left out. Where the
    editions spell a word differently (<i>ruunt</i> and <i>ruont</i>, <i>omnes</i> and <i>omnis</i>), the word keeps
    LASLA’s annotation. Lines 610 and 701 order two words differently and are matched by spelling. Where Greenough
    prints another word, the reader offers <i>candidate</i> analyses: those LASLA gives the same written form
    elsewhere in the Aeneid, or for an unknown form with an enclitic, its stem and the enclitic. Words with no
    candidate show no analysis.
    <details>
      <summary>Greenough’s other readings</summary>
      <ul>
        {readings.map(({ line, word, lasla }) => <li>1.{line} <i lang="la">{word}</i> (LASLA <i lang="la">{lasla}</i>)</li>)}
      </ul>
    </details>
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>English</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Theodore C. Williams, <i>The Aeneid of Virgil</i> (Boston: Houghton Mifflin, 1910)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. The text is Perseus’s <code>phi0690.phi003.perseus-eng2</code> from the same pinned commit.
    Williams’s blank verse follows Greenough’s paragraphs, which Perseus numbers by their first Latin line; the
    paragraph at line 132 starts inside his line “he voiced this word: | ‘What pride of birth or power”, and is split
    there.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>Dictionary</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Henry S. Frieze, <i>Vergil’s Aeneid</i>, vocabulary revised by Walter Dennison (1902)</dt>
  <dd>
    As edited by Dickinson College Commentaries, <a href="https://dcc.dickinson.edu/vergil-aeneid-vocabulary">Vergil
    Aeneid vocabulary</a>, licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>.
    DCC built the list from LASLA’s lemmas, so each LASLA lemma has its entries. Where a lemma has several
    (<i>ut</i>: result, manner, question), each LASLA homograph is matched to the entry with the same number of
    occurrences in the Aeneid, and the word panel opens the entries for the clicked word’s class. The export is not
    versioned; the build checks it against the snapshot it was made with.
  </dd>
</dl>
